<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>

<chapter xml:id="ch-diff" xmlns:xi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XInclude">
  <title>Differentiation</title>

  <introduction>

		<p>
				In this chapter we shall be studying functions whose co-domain is the set of real numbers <m>\mathbb{R}</m>. Usually the domain will be <m>\mathbb{R}</m> as well although not always, as for instance with the function <m>y = \frac{1}{x}</m> which has domain <m>\mathbb{R} \backslash \{ 0 \}</m>. So we will not normally spell out the domain and co-domain of the functions we talk about. In any particular case the domain can be figured out by considering where the formula defining the function makes sense.
			</p>
  </introduction>

